  "query_preview": "(This evening through Sunday)...\nIssued at 134 PM EDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nOverview.\n\nA weak frontal boundary moving across Central Indiana will bring a \nbrief chance of showers late this afternoon and early evening before \nhigh pressure brings cooler and less humid air on Monday. Hot and \nhumid conditions will return quickly on Tuesday and Wednesday as an \nupper-level ridge builds across the Ohio Valley, driving high \ntemperatures into the lower 90s. Occasional showers and storms are \nexpected late Tuesday through the weekend with best chances Tuesday \nnight and again on Thursday.\n\nTonight Through Tuesday.\n\nThe aforementioned surface cold front will drift through Central \nIndiana this evening and will exit to the southeast during the \novernight hours. HREF guidance and HRRR show isolated to scattered \nshowers along the baroclinic zone through sunset, diminishing \nrapidly as surface heating is lost and mid-level PVA departs. \nModerate CAA in the 925-850mb layer behind the front will push lower \ndewpoint air across the region, while surface high pressure \nexpanding from the Upper Midwest builds into the Ohio Valley. \nEfficient radiational cooling under clearing skies will drop Sunday \nnight lows into the 50s across most locations. There may be some \nfog/low stratus across the Upper Wabash Valley but with the much \ndrier air, confidence is low.\n\nSurface high pressure across the region on Monday will result in \nbroad-scale subsidence, light northerly flow, and pleasantly cool \nconditions. Model sounding profiles show a dry tropospheric column \nwith low-level thermal mixing supporting daytime highs ranging from \nthe mid 70s northeast to the lower 80s in the lower Wabash Valley. \nAs the high shifts toward New England Monday night, return low-level \nsoutherly flow will initiate rapid moisture advection aloft. Mid-\nlevel height rises driven by an expanding 500mb ridge over the \nTennessee Valley will take hold by Tuesday. Strong thermal advection \nat 850mb will lift daytime maximum temperatures into the upper 80s \nto lower 90s on Tuesday, while dewpoints rise back into the upper \n60s. \n\nWednesday Through Sunday.\n\nThe synoptic pattern on Wednesday features a strong mid-level \nridging over the southeastern states and Tennessee Valley, holding \nlow-level baroclinicity and main storm tracks north over the Great \nLakes. Continued S/SW surface flow and low-level WAA will maintain \nhot and muggy conditions across Central Indiana, with highs in the \nlower to mid 90s. There are some signs that a frontal system Tuesday \nnight may sag south into central Indiana, but with the strength of \nthe aforementioned ridge, confidence is fairly low as to how far \nsouth the front and associated rain threats will go. While diurnal \nMUCAPE values may exceed 1500 J/kg, weak mid-level capping and \nbackground subsidence will keep convective risks isolated and \nunorganized through Wednesday afternoon. \n\nThe next best chance for rain arrives late Wednesday night into \nThursday as models show an upper trough ejecting out of the Plains \ntoward the Upper Mississippi Valley. An advancing surface cold front \nwill interact with an intensifying 40-50 kt 850mb LLJ, driving a \ndeep plume of moisture northward with PWAT values surpassing 1.6 to \n1.8 inches. Scattered showers and the potential for more organized \nthunderstorm clusters are likely at times Thursday into Friday along \nand ahead of the frontal boundary. Diurnal timing and cloud cover \nwill determine peak instability, though shear profiles remain \nsufficient to support convective organization. As the front slowly \npushes south by Sunday, post-frontal CAA and building surface high \npressure to the north will lower high temperatures into the upper \n70s to low 80s.",
